> phase are a red flag, and historically will not have high chances of success.
>
> Assuming we get to the end of next week and you have multiple strong
> proposals for your project, you and your co-mentors will need to confer
> privately and discuss which is the strongest of the applications you've
> received, and which one project you would ideally like to mentor.  In rare
> cases when there have been multiple strong applicants for a project,
> experienced mentors, and the top applications focused on sufficiently
> different aspects of the project idea, R has split a project into two and
> awarded two slots.  We do this reluctantly, and only with experienced mentors.
>
> For mentors new to GSoC, one of the things I've observed is that new
> mentors consistently underestimate how much time they will spend on GSoC
> over the course of the summer.  From Google's data, the average amount of
> time a mentor spends on GSoC is 10-15hrs per week.  New mentors, or mentors
> with projects that require a good deal of domain or technical expertise,
> often find themselves spending even more than this helping their students
> during the summer to get a successful outcome.
>
> For your postGIS project proposal, the email addresses of your co-mentors
> need to be added to the project idea page (and/or communicated directly to
> me off-list) so that I can invite them to the Google system for
> administering GSoC this year.
>
> The process around next Friday's application deadline is that Toby,
> Virgilio and I will start discussing mid next week the number of strong
> applications that we see for R GSoC projects. (Students, this is why your
> draft applications should be submitted ASAP and refined in concert with
> your mentors over the next five days) At some point (I need to check the
> schedule and the new admin system) we will need to request slots from
> Google.  Sometime after the student application period closes, Google will
> tell us how many slots we receive.
>
> Toby, Virgilio, and I will privately solicit input from mentors who have
> multiple strong proposals, but ultimately we need to assign students to
> slots.  Google (not us, and not our mentors) will announce to students
> whether their applications have been accepted or rejected on Apr 22.
>
> Regards,
>
> Brian
>
